    {
      "op": "replace",
      "path": "/graph/inferences/@R006/argument",
      "value": [
        {
          "id": "S001",
          "text": "Temporarily assume L1: D>0 and L2: 0<s<D for arbitrary real s. N001 gives H^s(E)=infinity. Fix any T>0. By the definition of H^s as the supremum of the scale contents, there exists a positive delta with H^s_delta(E)>T; otherwise their supremum would be at most the finite T. Moreover these contents are nondecreasing as scale decreases, since a cover admissible at a smaller scale is admissible at any larger one. Thus any such bound remains valid on decreasing its scale, so the contents become arbitrarily large at arbitrarily small scales. Record the existence F001 before making a choice.",
          "uses": [
            "N001"
          ],
          "uses_local": [],
          "objects": [
            "D",
            "s",
            "T",
            "E",
            "H"
          ],
          "produces": [
            {
              "id": "F001",
              "statement": "There exists a positive real v with H^s_v(E)>T.",
              "objects": [
                "H",
                "s",
                "E",
                "T",
                "v"
              ],
              "binders": [
                {
                  "symbol": "v",
                  "type": "positive real number"
                }
              ],
              "assumptions": [
                "L1",
                "L2"
              ]
            }
          ],
          "introduces": [
            "L1",
            "L2"
          ],
          "discharges": []
        },
        {
          "id": "S002",
          "text": "Choose delta from F001. N005 applies to the positive s and delta and gives H^(s+1)_delta(F)≥H^s_delta(E)>T. Taking the supremum defining H^(s+1)(F) yields H^(s+1)(F)>T. This is F002, independent of the chosen scale.",
          "uses": [
            "N005"
          ],
          "uses_local": [
            "F001"
          ],
          "objects": [
            "s",
            "delta",
            "E",
            "F",
            "H",
            "T"
          ],
          "produces": [
            {
              "id": "F002",
              "statement": "H^(s+1)(F)>T.",
              "objects": [
                "H",
                "s",
                "F",
                "T"
              ],
              "binders": [],
              "assumptions": [
                "L1",
                "L2"
              ]
            }
          ],
          "introduces": [],
          "discharges": []
        },
        {
          "id": "S003",
          "text": "Since F002 holds for the arbitrary positive finite T, H^(s+1)(F)=infinity. N001 applies with ambient dimension n+1 and exponent s+1>0, giving dim_H(F)≥s+1. Record F003.",
          "uses": [
            "N001"
          ],
          "uses_local": [
            "F002"
          ],
          "objects": [
            "T",
            "s",
            "n",
            "F",
            "H",
            "dim_H"
          ],
          "produces": [
            {
              "id": "F003",
              "statement": "dim_H(F)≥s+1.",
              "objects": [
                "F",
                "s",
                "dim_H"
              ],
              "binders": [],
              "assumptions": [
                "L1",
                "L2"
              ]
            }
          ],
          "introduces": [],
          "discharges": []
        },
        {
          "id": "S004",
          "text": "By N002, D is finite; under L1 it is positive. F003 holds for every s with 0<s<D. Such s can increase to D, so their s+1 have supremum D+1 and dim_H(F)≥D+1. Equivalently every real number below D+1 is below some s+1 with 0<s<D. Discharge L2 in this universal limiting argument and then L1, obtaining the complete conditional output.",
          "uses": [
            "N002"
          ],
          "uses_local": [
            "F003"
          ],
          "objects": [
            "D",
            "s",
            "F",
            "dim_H"
          ],
          "produces": [],
